From 30135bd51afe811730b181202651b4d2bef6fefa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 19:22:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(api): RENDER-02 - a floor with no photograph required,
 and 27% off the wire
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

/v1/render needs a room. The customer sends a picture of theirs and gets it back
with our floor in it — that is the flagship, and it is useless to anybody who
has not taken a photograph yet, which is everybody on the first page they land
on. There was nothing to sell to a retailer who just wants hickory herringbone
on their own catalogue page.

/v1/plate draws the floor on its own, under a real perspective camera in a
synthesised room, from a query string. One GET, no body, no upload. The answer
is a pure function of the parameters, so it goes out immutable with a one-year
max-age: put it in an <img src> and a CDN pays for each variant once rather than
once per visitor. It is the cheap tier and the one that sells the expensive one
— the first time one of their customers wants to see it in their own hallway,
that is /v1/render.

The shot is a parameter and not a constant, because a catalogue thumbnail and a
hero image want different framing from the same floor: eye height, pitch and
field of view, each bounded to angles that are still a room. Two recipes people
will want are in the README with the numbers that produce them, and both were
run before being written down: pitch past about 24 degrees puts the wall line
off the top of the frame and gives floor edge to edge, and a lower fov is a long
lens that fills the frame with boards.

WHAT THIS COST, AND WHAT IT DID NOT

The tiles exist twice. This service decodes PNG and nothing else — zero
dependencies is the design, node:zlib gives us PNG, and there is no webp decoder
in the standard library — so build-grain.py now writes PNG copies at a quarter
of the linear resolution into services/render-api/textures. That is not a
compromise: 256 texels across three inches is 85 texels to the inch, and the
largest plate this API will draw still minifies it. 1.3 MB for the set.

What is NOT duplicated is their size in inches, which is imported from
lib/floor-studio/grain.ts. Two copies of that number would be two chances for
the website's floor and the API's floor to be different floors — and this is not
hypothetical. The first version of the server-side loader scaled the declared
inches by the ratio of actual pixels to declared pixels, which is right for a
CROP and wrong for a RESIZE, and every floor this API rendered came out at
double the grain scale. It looked like a plausible floor. It was caught by
printing the number, not by looking at the picture, which is the only way it was
going to be caught, and there is now a test per species asserting the inches
survive the resize.

THE WIRE, WHERE THE OLD COMMENT WAS WRONG

png.ts said filtering "buys a few percent on photographic data and costs a pass
over every byte; the wire is not where this service spends its time". Measured
on a 900x675 hickory plate:

    RGBA, filter 0 (what shipped)   710 KB
    RGBA, filter 1 (Sub)            557 KB    -21%
    RGBA, filter 4 (Paeth)          613 KB    -14%
    RGB,  filter 1 (Sub)            518 KB    -27%

Twenty-one percent for one subtraction per byte, and another six for dropping a
channel the plate does not use. Sub beats Paeth because a rendered floor is
strongly correlated along a scanline — the boards run across the frame and the
light falls smoothly — and Sub is the cheaper of the two. The wire is not where
the service spends its TIME, but egress is metered and billed, and a quarter of
it is a quarter of a real cost. Both round-trip losslessly and a test says so.

The alpha channel is dropped only where the caller asserts the image is opaque.
A plate is, by construction, and a test checks it; a composite over somebody's
photograph is not, and does not pass it.

Also: the tiles are decoded at BOOT, not on the first request — 180ms paid once,
by us, rather than by whichever customer arrives first after a cold start. This
is what a warm machine is for and it is the argument fly.toml already makes.
/health reports how many decoded, because a tile that failed to load is not an
outage — that species renders from the catalogue's pigments instead, which is a
plainer floor and not a broken one — but it is a silently plainer product, so it
is reported rather than hidden. /v1/floors now carries a `photographed` flag per
species for the same reason: an integrator choosing what to show their own
customers is entitled to know which they are getting.

65 of 66 guards. parse-scan clean. Both service test files pass on Node alone,
which is still the whole test infrastructure this service has and still the
right amount.

